<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mohammad Reza Pahlavi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1919">1919</a>–<a href="/years/1980">1980</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Mohammad Reza Shah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mohammad Reza <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Pahlavi</a> was the last shah of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, reigning from 1941, when the Anglo-Soviet invasion forced his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Shah</a> to abdicate, until 1979. Restored to full power by the 1953 coup against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-mosaddegh">Mohammad Mosaddegh</a>, he launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-revolution">White Revolution</a> of land reform and modernisation in 1963 and ruled with the help of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savak">SAVAK</a> secret police. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-revolution">Islamic Revolution</a> drove him into exile in 1979.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mohammad-reza-pahlavi.jpg" alt="Mohammad Reza Pahlavi" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ghazarians · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mohammad_Reza_Pahlavi_1973_portrait_(4x5_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mohammad Reza was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> on 26 October 1919, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Khan</a>, a brigadier general in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-cossack-brigade">Persian Cossack Brigade</a>, later Reza Shah, and his second wife, Tadj ol-Molouk. In 1931 he was sent to Institut Le Rosey in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, the first Iranian heir apparent educated abroad. On 15 March 1939 he married Princess Fawzia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>; the marriage ended in divorce on 17 November 1948.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After the Anglo-Soviet invasion of 1941 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Shah</a> resigned, and on 17 September 1941 his son took the oath as shah. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> became a main route for British and later American aid to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. On 11 December 1946 the army, led by the Shah in person, entered Iranian Azerbaijan, and the Soviet-sponsored government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jafar-pishevari">Ja'far Pishevari</a> collapsed with little resistance.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-mosaddegh">Mohammad Mosaddegh</a> became prime minister in 1951 and nationalised the oil industry controlled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bp">Anglo-Iranian Oil Company</a>. He was overthrown in the 1953 coup, carried out by pro-monarchy forces with British and American involvement. In 1963 the Shah launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-revolution">White Revolution</a>, whose referendum let women vote. Clerics objected, and demonstrations in 1963 and 1964, centred on Qom, killed at least 200 people; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhollah-khomeini">Ruhollah Khomeini</a> was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> in October 1965. On 26 October 1967 the Shah took the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahanshah">Shahanshah</a> at a coronation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, and in October 1971 he marked 2,500 years of Persian monarchy since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After a newspaper article attacking Khomeini appeared on 7 January 1978, protests began. On 8 September 1978 troops shot dead at least 64 people in Jaleh Square. Shapour Bakhtiar became prime minister on 6 January 1979, and on 16 January the Shah left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>; the monarchy collapsed within a month, and Khomeini became supreme leader of the new Islamic republic. The Shah travelled through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahamas">Bahamas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, and died in exile in Egypt on 27 July 1980. Historians remain divided on his rule, some stressing repression and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autocracy">autocracy</a>, others economic growth and modernisation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mohammad Reza Pahlavi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1963 <b>Mohammad Reza Shah</b>’s White Revolution gave Iranian women the vote, and during the revolution of 1979 many of the rights the women’s movement had gained, such as the Family Protection Law, were systematically abolished.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/feminism">Feminism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>Rulers have come to see it: <b>Mohammad Reza Pahlavi</b> with Emperor Haile Selassie in 1968, and Queen Juliana and Prince Bernhard the next year.</blockquote><a href="/work/rock-hewn-churches-of-lalibela">Rock-hewn churches of Lalibela · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Mohammad Reza Shah</b> its themes of regicide and the incompetence of kings sat badly with the monarchy, and Ali Shariati opposed it for verses critical of Islam.</blockquote><a href="/work/shahnameh">Shahnameh · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>The Anglo-Soviet invasion of 1941 forced his abdication in favour of his son <b>Mohammad Reza Shah</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/pahlavi-iran">Pahlavi Iran</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.69,51.39&amp;year=1958" aria-label="Tehran on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5848 874 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mohammad Reza Pahlavi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6298 1144h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6298 1144h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.69,51.39&amp;year=1958">What was here in 1958? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
